# Day 46

**37km / 10.5 hours**

*Waihohonu Hut to Waimarino(the town formerly known as National Park*

The day began with more rain and an early start from some snoring in the hut room so we left at 7am during a nice dry spell. It didn't last long though as the rain set in. We were thinking about walking around Mount Ruapehu which is a nice but longer route but with the awful weather we decided to just head on the standard route out towards the town. This way went between Mount Ruapehu and Mount Ngauruhoe which was the mountain used as a stand-in for Mount Doom in Lord of the Rings.

![The track between the mountains](./mountain-track.avif)

This curved off before coming to an intersection where you could go to see the Tama lakes but we didn't have time to do that today and the weather made it unappealing, there were heaps of day walkers beyond that point though. There was also another intersection to go and see Taranaki Falls which was more interesting in the rain. 

[Lots of water coming down the waterfall]($video.asset('taranaki-falls.mp4'))

After that it was a decent stretch to get to Whakapapa Village which at least had a shelter and visitor centre to decompress and get ready for the next section. There was even a cart for a hot pie but we didn't want to linger there all day. The next section was really nice as it passed through an alpine bog but the track had boardwalk and the clouds had parted somewhat to give a spectacular view of Mount Ruapehu.

![The awesome mountain with snow in the distance](./ruapehu.avif)

After the track had an intersection away from the round the mountain track the quality deteriorated precipitously and the rain was causing it to be worse than boggy and basically just walking down a slippery stream. Progress became much more difficult as a result even though the rain had dissipated by this point. It was endless passing through the mud and water whether the track was in the bog or the bush it was never easy to traverse and I frequently slipped over and nearly fell. There was even a stream to cross which would normally be fairly comfortable but with the rain it was fast moving and deeper than expected so it was probably reckless to cross. 

After what seemed like forever in this section we eventually got to the campsite besides the road but even after that there was 7km or so on the road to get to the town and a nice hot shower and meal was too enticing to not do. While walking in on the road the rain came back heavier than ever and it was extremely dramatic walking in with the thunder and lightning in the distance. The blasts of light followed a bit later by the booming sounds rolling around were pretty awesome and I could appreciate despite soaking in the rain as I arrived at Waimarino.
